# Synergistic Effect of Excited State Property and Aggregation Characteristic of Organic Semiconductor on Efficient Hole‐Transportation in Perovskite Device.

## abstract
Intrinsic characteristics of organic semiconductor‐based hole transport materials (HTMs) such as facile synthesizability, energy level tunability, and charge transport capability have been highlighted as crucial factors determining the performances of perovskite photovoltaic (PV) cells. However, their properties in the excited state have not been actively studied, although PVs are operated under solar illumination. Here, the characteristics of organic HTMs in their excited state such as transition dipole moment can be a decisive factor that can improve built‐in potential of PVs, consequently enhancing their charge extraction property as well as reducing carrier recombination. Moreover, the aggregation property of organic semiconductors, which has been an essential factor for high‐performance organic HTMs to improve their carrier transport property, can induce a synergistic effect with their excited state property for the high‐efficiency perovskite PVs. Additionally, it is also confirmed that their optical bandgaps, manipulated to have their absorption in the UV region, are beneficial to block UV light that degrades the quality of perovskite, consequently improving the stability of perovskite PV in p–i–n configuration. As a proof‐of‐concept, a model system, composed of triarylamine and imidazole‐based organic HTMs, is designed, and it is believed that this strategy paves a way toward high‐performance and stable perovskite PV devices.

Intrinsic characteristics of organic semiconductor-based hole transport materials (HTMs) such as facile synthesizability, energy level tunability, and charge transport capability have been highlighted as crucial factors determining the performances of perovskite photovoltaic (PV) cells. However, their properties in the excited state have not been actively studied, although PVs are operated under solar illumination. Here, the characteristics of organic HTMs in their excited state such as transition dipole moment can be a decisive factor that can improve built-in potential of PVs, consequently enhancing their charge extraction property as well as reducing carrier recombination. Moreover, the aggregation property of organic semiconductors, which has been an essential factor for high-performance organic HTMs to improve their carrier transport property, can induce a synergistic effect with their excited state property for the high-efficiency perovskite PVs. Additionally, it is also confirmed that their optical bandgaps, manipulated to have their absorption in the UV region, are beneficial to block UV light that degrades the quality of perovskite, con­ sequently improving the stability of perovskite PV in p–i–n configuration. As a proof-of-concept, a model system, composed of triarylamine and imidazolebased organic HTMs, is designed, and it is believed that this strategy paves a way toward high-performance and stable perovskite PV devices.
## 1. Introduction
Since the first attempt of perovskite-­sensitized liquid-electrolyte solar cells with $3.8\%$ efficiency,[1] a variety of aspects of perovskite photovoltaic (PV) cells, ranging from materials to device architectures, have been widely investigated, consequently leading their single-junction efficiency to reach more than $25\%$ now.[2] Among various factors, the development of an efficient and stable interfacial layer, which can not only boost collection efficiency of charge carriers but also reduce their recombination within the device structures, through the fine-tuning of its energy level along with the enhancement of its intrinsic carrier transport property, has been focused on as one of the essentials for further improving their performances toward thermodynamic efficiency limit.[3–5]
As successful hole transport materials (HTMs) in perovskite PV devices, various inorganic p-type semiconductors such as $\mathrm{CuO}_{x},$ $\mathrm{NiO}_{x},$ and $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ have been widely studied due to their suitable energy levels and hole mobility together with their prominent stability.[5–8] On the other hand, organic semiconductor-based HTMs, having a superior low-temperature processability feasible to a flexible substrate,[9] have also attracted high interest, and poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS),[10] $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis[N,N-di(4-methoxy­phenyl)amino]- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD),[11] and poly[bis(4-phenyl)
(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA)[12] are widely utilized as HTMs for perovskite PV devices. Even though these organic semiconductor-based HTMs have showed great promise toward future flexible solar cell technologies, their drawbacks such as inherent low transport property, inferior stability originated from the dopant for better performance, and the complicated synthesis procedures are preventing further extension of these materials to the actual commercialization of perovskite PV technology. In this respect, the development of novel organic HTMs, which have a facile synthesizability (e.g., short reaction steps with conventional simple materials, uncomplicated structure-tunability with various functional groups, low-cost processability, and high yield), along with excellent charge transport capability (e.g., through efficient intermolecular interactions) even without dopants, has become a crucial issue in realizing high performance and stable perovskite PV devices.[13]
In addition to those inherent properties of organic HTMs, the importance of their characteristics at photo-excited state, which has been disregarded, recently begins to be recognized.[14,15] Because PV devices are always operated under a solar illumination condition, the status of organic HTM molecule at the excited state becomes effective during the device operation, and especially the transition dipole moment of organic molecule at photo-excited state was revealed to work beneficially, increasing the built-in potential of the PV devices. The positive effects of the transition dipoles were successfully demonstrated, but the studied materials were limited to the conventional organic semiconductors, of which dipole effects were not high enough,[15] or the amorphous characteristics of the organic molecules were not advantageous to efficient charge transportation.[14]
In this work, we synthesize triphenylamine and imidazolebased small molecule HTMs, which have high dipole moment in the excited state. Moreover, to maximize their high polarity effect at the excitation, the molecules are designed to have an extended lifetime at the excitation by tautomerization through a proton-transfer, increasing the probability for the molecules to be in the excited state. Our results show that the organic HTM molecule having a higher dipole moment at the excitation is advantageous to provide higher built-in potential to the PV devices, consequently enhancing their charge extraction property along with the reduced carrier recombination. Furthermore, by enhancing their intermolecular aggregation characteristic, an additional performance improvement is feasible through better charge transportation. It is also shown that the organic molecules having strong absorption in UV region are beneficial to enhance the stability of PV device in p–i–n configuration by blocking the UV light from entering into the perovskite layer.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Design of Organic Hole Transport Materials
The molecular structure of a triphenylamine- and imidazolebased small molecule, 4-(diphenylamino)-2-(1,4,5-triphenyl1H-imidazol-2-yl)phenol (denoted as DPI hereafter), designed in this work, is presented in Figure 1a. For improving the hole transporting property, electron-rich triphenylamine moiety having high hole mobility is utilized, and electron-deficient imidazole moiety is added to form electron donor (D)–acceptor (A) structure that can tune their optical and electrical properties suitable for the HTM of perovskite PV devices.[16] Additionally, its asymmetric design is advantageous to provide high dipole moment. Meanwhile, another organic molecule, of which the triphenylamine moiety is replaced into dinaphthylamine unit that can provide the extended conjugation length with an increased structural rigidity, 4-(dinaphthalen-2-ylamino)-2-(1,4,5-triphenyl1H-imidazol-2-yl)phenol (denoted as DNI hereafter), is further designed to enhance its charge transport property as an HTM through better intermolecular interaction between adjacent molecules (Figure  1b). Its increased conjugation length is also beneficial to provide higher dipole moment than that of DPI.
The synthetic procedures of the molecules are described in the Experimental Section, General Synthetic Procedure, Supporting Information, and Figure S1, Supporting Information. The detailed experimental information about the synthesized molecules such as $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR, $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR, and LCMS are presented in Figures S2–S9, Supporting Information. With commercially available starting materials, all molecules were synthesized in a simple two-step reaction method with high reaction yield of $72\%$ . The estimated synthetic costs of DPI and DNI are only 1/25 and $1/12$ of the high purity spiroOMeTAD, based on a model proposed by Osedach et  al.[19] Energy levels of the designed molecules are estimated by cyclic voltammetry (CV) results (Figure S10, Supporting Information), along with the absorption edge information (Figure S11, Supporting Information), and their deep HOMO levels and shallow LUMO levels are proper to the hole-transport and electron-block from the perovskite photoactive layer, respectively (Figure  1e). In the meantime, the optical bandgaps of designed organic molecules are confirmed to have strong absorption only in the UV region under $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information), which is beneficial to applying them as HTMs of p–i–n PV structure by maximizing the amount of photon into the photoactive layer.
## 2.2. Characteristics of Organic Molecules at Photo-Excitation
a)Ground state dipole moment of enol form (theoretical value by DFT, unit: D); b)Excited state dipole moment of enol form (theoretical value by DFT, unit: D); c)Excited state dipole moment of keto form (theoretical value by DFT, unit: D); d)Fluorescence anisotropy results (experimental)
## 2.3. Aggregation of Organic Molecules
The aggregation behavior of organic molecules in the thin film state was analyzed by 2D grazing-incidence X-ray diffraction (2D GIXRD). Different from the thin film of DPI molecule that does not show noticeable diffractions (Figure 1c), the thin film of DNI molecule, having rigid naphthyl functional groups in the triarylamine moiety, shows strong diffraction peaks, indicating their strong intermolecular stacking in the solid-state thin film (Figure  1d). Those intermolecular $\pi$ -orbital overlaps are expected to facilitate intermolecular charge transport within the thin film of DNI molecule as an HTM.[28,29] The enhanced carrier transport property was confirmed by the hole mobility of the organic molecules. Figure 1f shows the current density– voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of hole-only devices having the following configuration, $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{NiO}_{x}/$ organic $\mathrm{HTM}/\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Ag}$ , at dark condition, and the hole mobility of DNI $(3.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ , calculated by the Mott–Gurney law about a space-charge limited current: $J=9\varepsilon_{\scriptscriptstyle0}\varepsilon_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{T}}\mu V^{2}/8L^{3}$ , where $\varepsilon_{\scriptscriptstyle0}\varepsilon_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{T}}$ is the permittivity of the component, $\mu$ is the carrier mobility, and $L$ is the thickness,[30] is about four times higher than that of DPI $(8.1\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ ).
## 2.4. Induced Dipole Moment of Organic Molecules
Although molecules have high dipole moments, their effects within tens of nanometers thick film are not valid enough because these molecules are randomly distributed within the film, of which dipole effects are cancelled out, accordingly (Figure S15, Supporting Information). Therefore, to utilize dipole moment effect of a polar molecule at the device interface, a few nanometers thick or even monolayer-type molecules have been commonly applied.[31] However, as for the perovskite PV device structures, a strong built-in electric field is generated within the device by the work-function difference of opposite electrodes at short-circuit condition, which is originally introduced for the efficient charge extraction, and this electric field can produce an electronic polarization in the molecule (e.g., variation of electron clouds), generating induced dipole moments (e.g., polarizability $\times$ electric field) even within tens of nano­ meters thick film, aligned to the electric field.[32] These induced dipole moments in the HTM can positively function, increasing the work-function of anode (e.g., a case of p–i–n structure is depicted in Figure 1g), and this enhances the built-potential of the PV devices, which will be discussed with the capacitancevoltage $(C-V)$ characteristics of the devices later. The increased built-in potential consequently enhances the charge extraction property of PV devices, improving their performances.
The generation of induced dipole moment within the organic thin film by the electric field was confirmed by organic field-effect-transistors (OFETs), which embedded the organic thin film as a gate dielectric (Figure 3g). If the induced dipole moments would be efficiently generated within the organic thin film by the vertical electric field from the gate voltage, those would be helpful to accumulate additional charges at the interface between the semiconductor (e.g., pentacene) and dielectric (e.g., organic layer with $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ ), consequently decreasing the threshold voltage $(V_{\mathrm{T}})$ of OFETs and increasing their drain current $\left(I_{\mathrm{DS}}\right)$ .[33,34] Figure 3a,c,e confirms that $V_{\mathrm{T}}$ of OFET decreases from 32.7 to $30.3\mathrm{~V~}$ after inserting DPI layer and this can be further decreased to $30.0\mathrm{V}$ after introducing a DNI layer having higher dipole moment instead of DPI. Moreover, to elucidate the alignment effect of excited state dipole moment, UV light was further illuminated, and we observed additional $5.3\mathrm{~V~}V_{\mathrm{T}}$ decrease (from 30.3 to $25.0\mathrm{V}$ in Figure 3c) from the DPI-added OFET and $5.5\mathrm{~V~}V_{\mathrm{T}}$ decrease (from 30.0 to $24.5~\mathrm{V}$ in Figure 3e) from the DNI-added OFET. For reference, transfer characteristics ( $(I_{\mathrm{DS}}$ vs $V_{\mathrm{GS}})$ ) of OFETs, forward and reverse scanned, are in Figure S16, Supporting Information. Additionally, those OFETs showed enhancement of $I_{\mathrm{DS}}$ under the light-illumination (DPI-added OFET: $32.4\%\uparrow$ in Figure 3d DNI-added OFET: $40.0\%\uparrow$ in Figure 3f ).
The electric field-oriented excited state dipole moment could be further evaluated by the variation of PL anisotropy under the electric field. The chromophore molecules in the solid-state thin film can barely rotate and have negligible reabsorption, and thus the relaxation of anisotropy reflects the arrangement of excited state dipoles. Figure 2c shows that anisotropy values of both DPI and DNI thin films gradually decrease with the enhancement of electric field, indicating fast arrangement of the excited state dipole moments, and the DNI film has larger anisotropy variation than the DPI film.
## 2.5. Perovskite PV Cell Performances with Organic Hole Transport Materials
In order to evaluate the enhanced excited state dipole moment effect of the designed organic molecules, together with their aggregation property, p–i–n configuration perovskite PV cells, utilizing $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}(\mathrm{FA}_{0.85}\mathrm{MA}_{0.15})_{0.95}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.85}\mathrm{Br}_{0.15})_{3}$ perovskite with $6\mathrm{\mol}\%$ excess KI for defect-passivation,[17] were prepared $\mathrm{(ITO/NiO}_{x}/$ organic HTM/perovskite/[6,6]-phenyl $\mathrm{C}_{61}$ butyric acid methyl ester [PCBM]/bathocuproine [BCP]/Ag). Figure 4a is the cross-sectional scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of a PV device. For reference, $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ was further utilized due to its advantages such as high open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ and stability.[35,36] Figure S17, Supporting Information, shows that the morphological variation of perovskite layer depending on the underlying HTM is not noticeable.
$J{-}V$ characteristics of the devices with different HTMs are summarized in Figure  4b. The PV device without organic HTM has a PCE of $17.6\%$ with a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $21.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.03\mathrm{~V},$ and a fill factor (FF) of 0.78. Whereas, the efficiency of PV cell with DPI organic HTM is enhanced to $18.6\%$ $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $22.9\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.04\mathrm{V},$ and FF of 0.78), and that of PV with DNI organic HTM having a higher excited dipole moment as well as superior aggregation property is further increased to $20.3\%$ $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $23.8~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.09\mathrm{V},$ and FF of 0.78). The improved device performances with the organic HTMs are also confirmed by external quantum efficiency (EQE) signals of PV devices (Figure S18, Supporting Information). The average PV cell performances are summarized in Table S1, Supporting Information. Figure 4c shows that the hysteretic behavior of PV device with the DNI organic HTM depending on the scan direction and rate is negligible and Figure S19, Supporting Information, represents that the PCEs at the maximum power point almost correspond to those obtained by $J{-}V$ characteristics, indicating the stable operation of the PV devices.
As mentioned earlier, the induced dipole moments of the organic molecules in thin film, especially those in excited state having higher value, are beneficial to improve the built-in potential of the PV devices. Figure 5 represents Mott–Schottky plots $(C^{-2}-V)$ of the devices with and without organic HTMs from the $C{-}V$ measurement. It is confirmed that the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of the device increases from 0.96 to $0.98{\mathrm{~V~}}$ after adding DPI layer, and this further increase to $1.01\mathrm{V}$ with DNI layer having higher dipole moment. More importantly, $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of the device without organic HTM is not changed at all under UV light (illuminated from ITO-side to excite organic HTM), whereas that of the device with DPI increases from 0.98 to $1.01\mathrm{V}$ and that of device with DNI having higher dipoles further increases from 1.01 to $1.06~\mathrm{V},$ representing the enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of the PV devices by the photo-excited state dipole moment effect. For reference, $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ values were calculated with the following equation: $C^{-2}=2(V_{\mathrm{bi}}{-}V)(A^{2}e\varepsilon\varepsilon_{\mathrm{o}}N_{\mathrm{A}})^{-1}$ , where $A$ is the active device area and $N_{A}$ is the doping concentration.[37,38]
Meanwhile, the organic HTMs have strong absorption in UV region, and this is beneficial to block the UV light that can degrade perovskite layer. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in Figure 4e show that $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ -related peaks are generated from the perovskite prepared on ITO/bare $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ after $12\mathrm{~h~}$ continuous UV light-illumination ( $365~\mathrm{nm}$ , $15\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ , and illumination from ITO-side in inert condition), whereas those are not observed from the perovskite on ITO/DNI-cast $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ even after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ illumination (Figure  4f). Consequently, the DNI-added perovskite PV device shows superior stability, maintaining over $90\%$ efficiency of its initial performance during continuous light exposure for $500\mathrm{~h~}$ in air at room temperature (1 Sun, non-UV-filtered and $50\ \mathrm{nm\Al_{2}O_{3}}$ -encapsulated), to the PV cell without organic HTM, retaining only about $20\%$ of initial PCE after the same light-illumination condition (Figure 4d).
## 2.6. Hole Extraction Characteristics at the Interface
The improved hole extraction characteristic at the interface between HTM and perovskite could be investigated by the time-resolved PL (TRPL) signal of perovskite on various HTMs using time-correlated single photon counting (TCSPC) system. PL decay curves of perovskite layers on various types of HTMs (Figure 4g) show that the average PL lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{avg}})$ of the perovskite cast on DNI layer, having the highest excited state dipole moment along with strong molecular aggregation property, is the smallest $(\mathrm{NiO}_{x}/\mathrm{DNI}/$ perovskite: $2.8<\mathrm{NiO}_{x}/\mathrm{DPI}$ /perovskite: $4.6<\mathrm{NiO}_{x}/$ perovskite: 8.1 ns), which means non-radiative recombination can be minimized by inserting DNI organic HTM layer. The average PL lifetimes were calculated by fitting the experimental data to biexponential decay functions (Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ and Table S2, Supporting Information). In the meantime, the charge extraction behavior in the full device structure can be further studied by transient photocurrent (TPC) measurements. The carrier extraction lifetime of DNI HTM-added PV device, measured by TPC measurement at short-circuit condition, is the shortest in all range of illumination intensity ranging from 0.01 to $1\sin$ (Figure  4h), indicating the efficient charge extraction with the reduced recombination in the DNI-added device.
## 3. Conclusion
The properties of organic HTMs in their excited state have not been actively investigated as a decisive factor for the performances of perovskite PV cells. However, in this work, we show that the characteristic of organic HTMs in their excited state such as transition dipole moment can be a critical factor that can improve the built-in potential of PV devices, consequently enhancing their charge extraction property as well as reducing carrier recombination. Moreover, an aggregation property of organic semiconductors, which has been a crucial factor of high performance organic HTMs to improve their carrier transport property, can induce a synergistic effect with their excited state property for the high-efficiency perovskite PV devices. Additionally, it is also confirmed that the absorption property of organic HTM, manipulated to be in UV region, can be also utilized to block the UV light that degrades the perovskite photoabsorber, consequently improving the stability of perovskite PV in p–i–n configuration.
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis and Characterization of Organic Hole Transport Materials: All chemicals and solvents were bought from Sigma-Aldrich and were utilized without further purification step. A drying process was thoroughly carried out in a convection oven before use for all the glass wares, syringes, needles, and magnetic stirrer bars. Supporting Information shows general synthetic procedures. Thin layer chromatography (TLC) with commercial TLC plates (silica gel 60 F254, Merck Co.) was conducted for monitoring the reactions. The authors proceeded with silica gel column chromatography with silica gel 60 (particle size $0.0063-0.200~\mathsf{m m}$ , Merck). Oxford 400  MHz NMR spectrometer and Oxford $700~\mathsf{M H z}$ spectrometer were used for $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR and $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ NMR spectra, respectively. The chemical shifts $(\delta)$ and coupling constant (J) were described in ppm and Hertz. JEOL (JMS-AX505WA) mass spectrometer was utilized to analyze mass spectra, and elemental analysis was carried out by using a CE Instrument (EA1110). Absorbance spectra were obtained by UV-2600 UV-Vis spectrophotometer (SHIMADZU) (200–900  nm wavelength). A potentiostat/galvanostat model 273A (Princeton Applied Research) was utilized for CV analysis. Tetra- $\cdot n$ -butylammonium tetrafluoroborate in dichloromethane $(0.7~\mathsf{m})$ was used for a supporting electrolyte. The scan speed and vertex potential were $\mathsf{l}00\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ and $2.5\:\mathrm{V},$ respectively.
Perovskite Preparation: Methylammonium bromide (MABr) and formamidinium iodide (FAI) were obtained from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp and GreatCell Solar Company, respectively. Lead bromide $(\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ , $98\%$ , cesium iodide (CsI, $99.9\%$ ), lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $99\%$ ), BCP $(96\%)$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, anhydrous), $N,N^{\prime}$ - dimethylformamide (DMF, anhydrous), and chlorobenzene (CB, anhydrous) were from Sigma-Aldrich. PCBM was purchased from Nano-C. All chemicals were used without further purification. $\mathsf{l}.4\mathsf{m}$ metal lead salts $(0.75~\mathsf{~M~}\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ and $0.85\mathrm{~\textmu~}\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ ) and $1.3~\mathsf{m}$ organic cations $(0.75~\mathsf{m}$ MABr and $0.85~\mathsf{m}$ FAI) were mixed in a DMF/DMSO solvent (4:1 volume ratio). $\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}(\mathsf{F A}_{0.85}\mathsf{M A}_{0.15})_{0.95}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.85}\mathsf{B r}_{0.15})_{3}$ was the composition of perovskite, utilized in this work, and $6\%$ mol KI was doped $(34\upmu\up L$ CsI and KI solution in DMSO $(2~\mathsf{m})$ was added to the perovskite solution).
Device Fabrication: $35\%$ HCl and zinc powder were used to etch ITO substrate. Acetone, isopropyl alcohol, and deionized water were used for cleaning. The substrate was treated with oxygen plasma for 15 min. ${\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}$ nanoparticle (NP) solution, dissolved in DI water, was filtered by a PTFE filter $(0.45~\upmu\mathsf{m})$ . ${\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}$ thin film layer was prepared by spincasting the solution on the ITO (2000 rpm for 20 s) and annealed at $\mathsf{l}70^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $15\mathrm{\min}$ in air. The thickness of $\mathsf{N i O}_{x}$ layer was around $20~\mathsf{n m}$ . The synthesis procedure of $\mathsf{N i O}_{x}$ NP is explained elsewhere.[39] DPI and DNI were dissolved in CB $(20\ m g\ m L^{-1})$ and spin-cast on ${\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}$ layer (2000  rpm for $20\ s)$ ). The thickness of organic HTL layer was around $20\ n m$ . The perovskite photoabsorber was formed on the HTM by spin-casting. $700-\upmu\upiota$ mixed perovskite solution was dropped and spun at 1000  rpm for 10  s and 6000  rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}.~700~\upmu\mathsf{L}$ anti-solvent (ethyl acetate) was dropped at the last $5s$ . The films were annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . The thickness of perovskite layer was around $500~\mathsf{n m}$ . PCBM $(20\ m g\ m L^{-1}$ in CB) was cast at 1000 rpm for $20~\mathsf{s}$ (around $30~\mathsf{n m}_{\iota}^{\prime}$ ). The films were annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10\mathrm{\min}$ . BCP ( $0.5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ in ethanol) was spin-cast at 4000 rpm for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ (under $70\ n m$ ). Finally, $\mathsf{A g}$ electrode having around $\mathsf{100~n m}$ thickness was thermally deposited.
Characterization: AM $\mathsf{I}.5\mathsf{C}$ illumination condition with $\mathsf{l o o\ m w c m}^{-2}$ was utilized for PV cell measurement (PEC-L01, Peccell Technologies, Inc.). A NREL-certified Si photodiode with a KG5 was utilized to calibrate intensity with reduced spectral mismatch. $J-V$ curves were scanned with a $\dot{0}.1\vee{\mathsf{s}}^{-1}$ condition. An ABET Technology 10500 solar simulator and a SPECTRO Mmac-200 were utilized for the EQE. The hole-only devices for estimating the hole mobility of organic HTM were prepared with the following structure: ${\mathsf{I T O}}/{\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}/$ organic HTL/ ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}/{\mathsf{A g}}$ . ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}$ having a high work-function was utilized for blocking electrons from the electrode. Ultima III (Rigaku) diffractometer (copper $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation) was utilized for XRD patterns. Step scanning was at angular intervals of $0.08^{\circ}$ from $10^{\circ}$ to $90^{\circ}$ . The samples had the following configuration: ${\mathsf{I T O}}/{\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}/$ perovskite or ${\mathsf{I T O}}/{\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}/$ organic HTM/perovskite. For 2D GIXRD measurement, 2D scan was conducted by using Rigaku SmartLab $(9\mathrm{kw})$ at a scan speed of 10. Data were collected at an angle of $5^{\circ}$ to $80^{\circ}$ . Fluorometer (F7000, Shimazu, Japan) was used for PL anisotropy (wavelength range of $300{-}700\mathsf{n m})$ ). $375\mathsf{n m}$ wavelength was used to excite the organic HTMs. Fluorescence anisotropy decrease by the electric field was analyzed by the following structure: $|\mathsf{T O}/\mathsf{N i O}_{x},$ /organic HTM/ZnO/Al. To prepare OFETs, DPI or DNI $(70\ n m)$ was spin-cast on $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ (n-doped $\mathsf{S i}/300\mathsf{n m}\mathsf{S i O}_{2})$ substrates, and ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ layer $(20\ n m)$ ) was added using atomic layer deposition at $700~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ at a deposition rate of $\mathsf{1.3-l.6~\AA}$ cycle−1. As an active layer, pentacene was formed using thermal evaporator. Source and drain were defined by Au. The channel width (W) and lengths (L) were $\mathsf{~l~}\mathsf{m m}$ and $30~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , respectively. Xe lamp with a monochromator was used to illuminate OFETs. TRPL spectra were obtained utilizing a commercial TCSPC system (FluoTime 200, PicoQuant). A picosecond diode laser of $375\ \mathsf{n m}$ (LDH-P-C-375, PicoQuant) was utilized to excite the sample. The emission peak of perovskite at $770\ n m$ was utilized to measure PL decay curves. For TPC signals, a small perturbed bias via a nanosecond OPO laser system (INDI-40-10, Spectra-Physics) from Nd:YAG laser was generated and a Xe lamp (LS-150-XE ABET) was utilized for background. The PV devices were connected to a digital oscilloscope (DSO-X 3054A, Agilent). 50  Ω input impedance was installed for short-circuit condition.
